{
  "title": "Home, sweet home",
  "title_target": "Ev ve eşyalar",
  "goal": "Describe where you live, and build new words yourself with the suffixes *-lı*, *-sız*, *-ci* and *-lik*.",
  "bekal": [
    "naming rooms and furniture",
    "forming *şekerli*, *şekersiz*, *kitapçı*, *güzellik* from words you already know",
    "reading a rental ad without a dictionary"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Dialogue — Yeni daire",
      "intro": "Budi is looking for a room closer to campus. Emre goes with him to see a small apartment.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Emlakçı:",
          "text": "Buyurun, girin. İki artı bir, altmış metrekare.",
          "id": "Come in, please. Two bedrooms plus a living room, sixty square meters.",
          "literal": "please, enter-you(formal). two plus one, sixty square-meter."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "İki artı bir ne demek?",
          "id": "What does \"two plus one\" mean?",
          "literal": "two plus one what saying?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Emre:",
          "text": "İki oda ve bir salon. Türkiye'de hep böyle yazılır.",
          "id": "Two rooms and one living room. In Turkey it's always written like that.",
          "literal": "two room and one living-room. Turkey-in always like-this is-written."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Anladım. Mutfak nerede?",
          "id": "I see. Where's the kitchen?",
          "literal": "understood-I. kitchen where?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Emlakçı:",
          "text": "Şurada. Küçük ama pencereli, çok aydınlık.",
          "id": "Over there. It's small but it has a window — very bright.",
          "literal": "over-there. small but window-with, very bright."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Eşyalı mı, eşyasız mı?",
          "id": "Furnished or unfurnished?",
          "literal": "furniture-with question, furniture-without question?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Emlakçı:",
          "text": "Eşyalı. Yatak, dolap, masa, buzdolabı var.",
          "id": "Furnished. There's a bed, a wardrobe, a table and a fridge.",
          "literal": "furniture-with. bed, cupboard, table, ice-cupboard-its there-is."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Kira ne kadar?",
          "id": "How much is the rent?",
          "literal": "rent how much?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Emlakçı:",
          "text": "On sekiz bin lira, aidat dahil.",
          "id": "Eighteen thousand lira, building fees included.",
          "literal": "ten eight thousand lira, fee included."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Emre:",
          "text": "Su ve elektrik dahil mi?",
          "id": "Are water and electricity included?",
          "literal": "water and electricity included question?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Emlakçı:",
          "text": "Hayır, onlar ayrı. Ama internet bedava.",
          "id": "No, those are separate. But the internet is free.",
          "literal": "no, they separate. but internet free."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Düşüneceğim. Yarın haber veririm.",
          "id": "I'll think about it. I'll let you know tomorrow.",
          "literal": "will-think-I. tomorrow news give-I."
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**2+1 is the language of Turkish housing ads.** The first number is bedrooms, the second is living rooms (*salon*). *1+1* is a small studio, *3+1* a family home. You'll see it on every *kiralık* sign in a real-estate office window.",
        "**Eşyalı and eşyasız use two opposite suffixes.** *-lı* means \"with, having\"; *-sız* means \"without\" — like English *-ful* and *-less*. These two suffixes are the adjective factory of Turkish, and part of this unit's grammar.",
        "**Buzdolabı** = *buz* (ice) + *dolap* (cupboard) + *-ı*, with *p* softening to *b*. The noun + noun pattern from unit 4 forms almost every name for household items.",
        "**Haber veririm** uses the aorist for a light promise — \"I'll let you know\". Compare *haber vereceğim*, which sounds more binding. For everyday matters, the aorist is actually more polite because it's looser."
      ]
    }
  ],
